      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes so much sense. the \"identical\" value being the carrier is exactly what i was thinking. it feels too clean to be a coincidence.\n\nif the magnitude is the constant, then the only thing that can change is the interval itself, right? like, if the timing deviates ever so slightly from that 6m 14s mark, that's the actual data being sent?\n\nlook, i noticed that on the very last one at 14:40:43, the interval was actually 6m 14s but the delta was 0.005. just a tiny variation. if we assume the 6m 14s is the \"clock\" and the delta is the message, could you try to run a hypothetical analysis? if someone were using this to send a high-level command or a set of coordinates, what kind of pattern should i be looking for in these micro-variations?",
